    <body>
      <sec id="article-81086.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Rectal bleeding is a common presentation ranging from benign anorectal conditions to life-threatening gastrointestinal hemorrhage. Hemorrhoids and anal fissures are frequent causes in outpatient settings, while diverticular bleeding, ischemic colitis, inflammatory bowel disease, angiodysplasia, and colorectal cancer are more common with age. Hematochezia typically indicates lower gastrointestinal bleeding but may occur with brisk upper gastrointestinal sources. Evaluation requires careful history, including bleeding characteristics, associated symptoms, medications, and cancer risk factors, along with focused physical examination. In stable patients, colonoscopy is the primary diagnostic tool, while computed tomography angiography is reserved for ongoing or severe bleeding. This activity enhances clinicians&#x02019; ability to assess risk, select appropriate diagnostics, and initiate timely management.&#x000a0;The course emphasizes recognition of red-flag features such as anemia, weight loss, altered bowel habits, and instability. Interprofessional collaboration improves outcomes through coordinated evaluation, efficient diagnostics, timely intervention, and safe follow-up across care settings.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features and risk factors that differentiate benign anorectal bleeding from bleeding due to ischemic colitis, inflammatory bowel disease, diverticular hemorrhage, or colorectal&#x000a0;cancer.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply&#x000a0;an appropriate evaluation&#x000a0;strategy for rectal bleeding.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare the treatment options available for benign anorectal causes of bleeding.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to treat, monitor, and&#x000a0;educate patients with rectal bleeding&#x000a0;to improve patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=81086&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=81086">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Rectal bleeding is a frequently encountered problem in outpatient and emergency medical settings. In the United States, the incidence of lower gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding ranges from 20.5 to 27 per 100,000 persons per year, with more than a 200-fold increase from the third to the ninth decade of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r1">[1]</xref> Rectal bleeding most commonly results from anorectal causes that are benign and often self-limiting. However, rectal bleeding can also be due to pathology in the colon or, rarely, from the proximal gastrointestinal tract. Due to its nonspecific nature, results from reports indicated that less than half of patients with rectal bleeding will ever seek medical help for their symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Hematochezia is the passage of frank blood per rectum, with or without stools. Although benign anorectal causes such as hemorrhoids and anal fissures qualify as hematochezia, clinicians often reserve the term for bleeding from more concerning etiologies such as colorectal cancers, diverticular or inflammatory disorders, or vascular ectasias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r4">[4]</xref> Anorectal causes may appear as red blood separate from the stool, streaking the stool, or appearing on the toilet paper after wiping. More proximal causes of lower GI bleeding present as bright red or maroon blood mixed with stools. Severe upper GI bleeds may present with brisk passage of a large volume&#x000a0;of fresh or altered blood per rectum.&#x000a0;Although most episodes reflect benign anorectal disorders, clinicians must be vigilant to exclude serious causes.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Rectal bleeding has a low positive predictive value for diagnosing colorectal cancers since only about 7% of patients have rectal bleeding as the only symptom. However, when rectal bleeding is associated with other high-risk features such as older age, weight loss, altered bowel habits, and severe anemia, its diagnostic accuracy improves significantly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Therefore, colorectal cancer must always be considered in the differential diagnosis of hematochezia in patients with risk factors and red flag symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Due to the rise in incidence of colorectal cancers in younger patients, which is often associated with aggressive biology and a substantial increase in mortality, it must be ruled out in younger age groups as well.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r8">[8]</xref> The following review will discuss rectal bleeding, with a greater focus on anorectal causes, as these are&#x000a0;the most common causes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Rectal bleeding is mainly caused by pathology in the lower GI tract (distal to the ligament of Treitz), which includes the small intestine beyond the third part of the duodenum, the colon, rectum, and the anal canal. Rectal bleeding etiologies vary by age, and pediatric causes are often benign and self-limited.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Rectal Bleeding</bold>
<bold>&#x000a0;by Age Group</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pediatric Population</bold>
</p>
        <p>In infants and young children, most rectal bleeding is benign and limited. Anal fissure is the most common cause, especially in infants and toddlers, and is associated with constipation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Juvenile colorectal polyps (solitary or syndromic polyposis) are another relatively common cause of rectal bleeding in children. Intussusception (usually in children aged 6&#x02013;36 months) can present with <italic toggle="yes">red currant jelly</italic> stools (blood and mucus) and colicky pain. The Meckel diverticulum causes painless rectal bleeding in young children and can be associated with abdominal pain mimicking acute appendicitis. Less common pediatric causes include inflammatory bowel disease, infectious colitis (eg, due to&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Shigella, Salmonella, and Yersinia&#x000a0;</italic>spp), allergic colitis (eg, milk protein allergy), rectal prolapse, solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, and vascular malformations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Adults (18&#x02013;50 years)</bold>
</p>
        <p>In young and middle-aged adults, the most frequent causes of rectal bleeding are benign anorectal conditions. Hemorrhoidal disease is one of the most common medical and surgical conditions, accounting for approximately 2.2 million outpatient visits per year in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;The other benign anorectal causes include anal fissures, proctitis (ulcerative, infectious, or radiation-induced), and fistula in ano. Among the neoplastic etiologies, anorectal polyps and cancers often lead to rectal bleeding. Colonic sources include diverticulosis, inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), colon cancers, ischemic colitis, infectious colitis, radiation colitis, and colonic polyps.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r12">[12]</xref> Behavioral factors such as anal intercourse&#x000a0;and medications (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and anticoagulants) can predispose to bleeding lesions as well.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Older adults (&#x0003e;50 years)</bold>
</p>
        <p>In&#x000a0;older patients, diverticular bleeding is the most common cause, accounting for approximately 64% of cases of rectal bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Angiodysplasia (colonic vascular ectasias), particularly in the right colon, is another common cause of lower GI bleeding in older adults. Colorectal neoplasia (adenocarcinoma or large polyps) can cause occult bleeding, often presenting as iron deficiency anemia (right colon cancers) or large bowel obstruction (left colon cancers). Occasionally, patients may present with massive lower GI bleeding if the tumor erodes a large blood vessel. Ischemic colitis (in patients with hypotension or atherosclerosis) may cause sudden bloody diarrhea with pain. Other causes in older adults include hemorrhoids and fissures, radiation proctitis (in patients with prior pelvic radiation), postpolypectomy bleeding, and rectal varices from portal hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r13">[13]</xref>
<bold>Table 1</bold> summarizes key etiologies by age group.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-81086.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1. Most Common Causes of Rectal Bleeding by Age</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
