                <td style="width: 243.703px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Diverticulosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Angiodysplasia and angioectasias</p></list-item><list-item><p>Colorectal polyps and cancer</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ischemic colitis ()</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hemorrhoids and anal fissures</p></list-item><list-item><p>Radiation colitis or proctopathy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Postpolypectomy bleeding</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory&#x02013;induced or stercoral ulcers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rectal&#x000a0;varices</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dieulafoy lesions</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 305px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p><bold>Diverticulosis:</bold>&#x000a0;Acute, intermittent, large-volume bleeding, constipation, and abdominal pain</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Angiodysplasia:</bold>&#x000a0;Moderate to large volume bleeding without associated symptoms</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Ischemic colitis:</bold>&#x000a0;Abdominal pain, systemic features, and bloody stool</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Radiation:</bold>&#x000a0;History of pelvic radiation therapy for rectal, prostate, or gynecologic cancers</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Rectal varices:</bold>&#x000a0;Fresh, painless bleeding, associated with esophageal varices and a history of chronic liver disease</p></list-item><list-item><p><bold>Dieulafoy lesions:</bold>&#x000a0;Intermittent moderate to massive lower GI bleeding without associated symptoms&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Small Intestinal Bleeding</bold>
</p>
        <p>Small intestinal causes account for approximately 5% to 10% of rectal bleeding and vary between age groups. Small intestinal etiologies include vascular lesions, Crohn disease, small bowel tumors, and nonspecific enteritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Upper GI Sources of Rectal Bleeding&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Upper GI bleeding, which is commonly due to peptic ulcer disease, esophageal varices, and malignant neoplasms, typically presents with black tarry stools. Hematochezia may occasionally result from upper GI causes if the bleeding is large-volume&#x000a0;and cannot be converted to acid hematin by gastric secretions. Concurrent hematemesis and hematochezia suggest an upper GI source of bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r16">[16]</xref>
<bold>
</bold><bold>Bleeding Disorders and Coagulopathies</bold></p>
        <p>When evaluating an individual for GI bleeding, it is also essential to consider other underlying factors that may contribute to the acute presentation. These include excessive anticoagulation and bleeding diatheses such as hemophilia, von Willebrand syndrome, vitamin K deficiency, and thrombocytopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Rectal bleeding represents a substantial healthcare burden with an average incidence of 20.4 to 27 per 100,000 persons per year in the United States, with more than a 200-fold increase from the third to the ninth decade of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Population-based studies estimate that 13% to 34% of adults report rectal bleeding at some point, though fewer than half seek medical attention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r20">[20]</xref> The prevalence of specific causes varies by age, and the incidence of rectal bleeding is the highest in older adults. In children, GI bleeding (upper or lower) is less common, with an estimated prevalence of approximately 6%. Hemorrhoids and fissures predominate in middle-aged adults. In contrast, diverticular bleeding, colitis due to ischemia, inflammation, and infections, colorectal malignant neoplasms, and vascular lesions such as angiodysplasia are more common in older adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Diverticulosis is the most common cause of lower GI bleeding in Western Europe and the United States, but it is less common in Asia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Comorbid&#x000a0;conditions such as cardiovascular disease, cirrhosis, renal disease, diabetes mellitus, and malignant neoplasms increase the incidence and severity of lower GI bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a population-based study of over 200,000 patients showed diverticular bleeding (33.1%) as the most common cause, followed by hemorrhoidal disease (20%), colorectal polyps (13.1%), colorectal malignant neoplasms&#x000a0;(8.2%), ischemic colitis (6.6%), and angiodysplasia (6%). The other causes were noninfectious colitis, inflammatory bowel disease, anal fissure, solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, and radiation colitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Lower GI bleeds&#x000a0;can be categorized as&#x000a0;massive, moderate, or occult, depending on the severity of bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r24">[24]</xref> The bleeding can be due to erosion of a blood vessel in the wall of the colon or rectum secondary to diverticulosis, colorectal cancer, and inflammatory or infectious conditions. Tumors and polyps also tend to outgrow their blood supply, leading to necrosis and bleeding. Angiogenesis&#x000a0;in&#x000a0;malignant neoplasms&#x000a0;can also lead to profuse bleeding&#x000a0;due to immature blood vessels that lack adequate fibroblasts and collagen.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Massive bleeding usually occurs in patients older than 65 years with multiple comorbidities and presents as a large volume of fresh or altered blood per rectum. Patients are generally hemodynamically unstable and anuric. The causes include diverticulosis (5%-42%), colitis due to ischemia, infections, or radiation (6%-18%), malignant neoplasms (3%-11%), vascular ectasias (0-3%), rectal varices (1%-8%), and inflammatory bowel diseases (2%-4%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Massive bleeding carries a high risk of mortality of up to&#x000a0;21%.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Moderate bleeding can occur at any age and presents as hematochezia or melena. The patient with moderate bleeding is usually hemodynamically stable. The differential diagnosis includes&#x000a0;neoplasia, inflammation, infection, benign anorectal problems, and congenital abnormalities.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occult bleeds&#x000a0;can also present at any age. The&#x000a0;etiologies include inflammatory, neoplastic, and congenital conditions. Patients typically appear well and are hemodynamically stable.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>History</bold>
</p>
        <p>Detailed history-taking and a thorough physical examination are essential to diagnose the cause of rectal bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;The consultation should focus on the onset, duration, amount, frequency, and passage of clots. Differentiation between fresh (bright red) and old blood (maroon or tarry) is also essential. Associated symptoms such as abdominal pain, weight loss, altered bowel habits, tenesmus, spurious diarrhea, painful defecation, and&#x000a0;a previous history of any pelvic surgical procedure, abdominal, or pelvic radiation should be included. Patients should also be asked about anorectal trauma and high-risk sexual behavior, including anal intercourse, fever, discharge, and rashes. A comprehensive review of the patient's comorbidities and medications is necessary. Special attention should be given to comorbidities that may contribute to bleeding tendencies or those that require the patient to take anticoagulants, antiplatelet agents, or nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, such as a prosthetic valve, rheumatic or ischemic heart disease, or atrial fibrillation.</p>
        <p>Rectal bleeding associated with anal or perianal pain is often suggestive of an anorectal cause. The most common differential is an anal fissure, which is usually associated with a history of constipation, straining, and passage of hard stools. Etiologies like perianal Crohn disease, tuberculosis, and sexually transmitted infections should be considered in patients with atypical or recurrent anal fissures. The other causes of painful rectal bleeding include carcinoma of the anal canal (squamous cell carcinoma or malignant melanoma), malignant fistula in ano, thrombosed, prolapsed, or strangulated hemorrhoids, complex fistula in ano, and solitary rectal ulcer syndrome. Patients who have completed treatment for rectal malignant neoplasms and present with rectal bleeding should be evaluated for local recurrence, anastomotic stricture, and radiation proctitis.</p>
        <p>The most common cause of painless fresh rectal bleeding during defecation in middle-aged and older adults is hemorrhoids. Hemorrhoids may be described as soft, painless protrusions in the anal canal that become engorged during straining and either reduce spontaneously or require manual reduction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;The associated symptoms include constipation, pruritus ani, anal discharge, sensation of incomplete evacuation, and perianal discomfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Conversely, patients with ischemic, inflammatory, or radiation colitis present with generalized abdominal pain, tenderness, guarding, and features of peritonitis. Similarly, diverticular bleeding can be associated with abdominal discomfort, chronic constipation, and recurrent symptoms due to diverticular complications such as diverticulitis and intrabdominal sepsis.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>The physical examination should begin with assessing the hemodynamic status and features of systemic inflammatory response syndrome. General physical examination includes pallor in case of severe anemia secondary to massive or chronic rectal bleeding; icterus and lymphadenopathy suggestive of metastatic colorectal malignant neoplasms; eye signs and skin changes in inflammatory bowel disease; rashes in infectious colitis, signs of liver failure in rectal variceal bleeding, and hyperpigmentation in radiation colitis or proctitis. A focused examination for rectal bleeding should include an abdominal examination with assessment for pain, abdominal masses, intestinal obstruction, free fluid, and distention. These findings suggest etiologies such as diverticulosis, colorectal cancer, inflammatory bowel disease, and ischemic, infectious, or radiation colitis. The perineum should be examined to evaluate for thrombosed or prolapsed hemorrhoids, fissures, sentinel pile, external openings of complex fistula in ano, and carcinoma of the anal canal.</p>
        <p>Digital rectal examination is crucial for evaluating rectal bleeding and must be performed thoroughly. Anorectal polyps, malignant neoplasms, thrombosed internal hemorrhoids, radiation proctitis, solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, and complex fistula in ano can be readily diagnosed with a rectal examination. The examining digit should be checked for blood color, association with feces, and the presence of mucus or pus. Rectal examination should not be performed in patients with acute anal fissure, as this can be extremely painful and can induce vasovagal syncope. Rectal examination is also contraindicated in patients who are severely immunocompromised or on chemotherapy. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28269">Rectal Exam,</ext-link> for further information. Proctoscopy or anoscopy is generally performed after a rectal examination to diagnose anorectal conditions such as hemorrhoids, polyps, and malignant lesions. Videoproctoscopy is an office procedure that provides high-definition images of the anal canal and lower rectum and can be used to diagnose and follow up on various anorectal conditions that cause rectal bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r29">[29]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Any rectal bleeding event warrants evaluation based on clinical context and risk factors to determine which patients require hospitalization and which can be discharged for outpatient evaluation. The Oakland score is shown in <bold>Table 2</bold>. A score of 8 or less indicates that the patient is at low risk of rebleeding and requires therapeutic intervention (endoscopic, angiographic, or surgical) or hospital readmission within 28 days. Patients with a low risk, without ongoing bleeding, and with a recent colonoscopy with effective preparation that excluded malignant neoplasm and other causes of bleeding can be safely discharged for outpatient evaluation with 95% accuracy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 2:</bold> Oakland Score for&#x000a0;Identification of Low-Risk Patients With Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding&#x000a0;</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-81086.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Oakland score parameter</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Criteria and points (in parentheses)</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Age</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c; 40 years (0 points), 40-69 years (1 point), &#x02265; 70 years (2 points)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Sex</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Female (0), male (1)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Prior admission for lower GI bleed</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">No (0), Yes (1)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Rectal examination findings</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">No blood (0), blood (1)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Initial pulse (beats/min)</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c; 70 (0), 70-89 (1), 90-109 (2),&#x000a0; 110 (3)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Initial blood pressure, systolic (mm Hg)</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265; 160 (0), 130-159 (2), 120-129 (3), 90-119 (4), 50-89 (5)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Hemoglobin (g/dL)</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02265; 16.0 (0), 13.0-15.9 (4), 11.0-2.9 (8), 9.0-10.9 (13), 7.0-8.9 (17), 3.6-6.9 (22)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Blood Testing</bold></p>
        <p>A complete blood count should be done to assess the severity of bleeding and guide management. Prothrombin time, international normalized ratio, and partial thromboplastin time help assess any bleeding tendencies. The other blood investigations include serum creatinine, liver function tests, random blood glucose, and C-reactive protein levels, depending on the clinical presentation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;In patients with a history of anal receptive intercourse, testing for sexually transmitted infections (including gonorrhea and chlamydia) should be performed. <bold>Endoscopic Evaluation</bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Colonoscopy is the gold standard for evaluating rectal bleeding in hemodynamically stable patients. Colonoscopy should be recommended for all adults, regardless of other clinical symptoms, to identify colorectal or distal ileal pathology, especially in the absence of anal pathology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r33">[33]</xref> Colonoscopy can provide precise localization and diagnosis of bleeding sites, allow biopsies if necessary, and enable therapeutic measures such as polypectomy and various forms of hemostasis. Colonoscopy is best performed 24 to 72 hours after admission if bleeding has stopped, since urgent colonoscopy in the first 24 hours of hospitalization is challenging due to inadequate preparation. Results from studies showed that urgent colonoscopies reduce hospital stays. However, urgent colonoscopies also increase the risk of rebleeding and hospital readmission, without improving diagnostic accuracy, reducing the need for blood transfusions, or decreasing mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r36">[36]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with known diverticular disease who had negative colonoscopy results within 1 year of a rectal bleeding episode may not need another colonoscopy if the bleeding has stopped and the diagnosis is likely diverticulosis-related.&#x000a0;Only 3% to 6% of patients undergoing colonoscopy for acute rectal bleeding require therapeutic intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Colonoscopy preparation in the setting of rectal bleeding has traditionally been conducted with 4 to 6 L of a polyethylene glycol&#x02013;based solution over 3 to 4 hours. However, in an emergency setting, results from recent evidence showed that a split-dose prep with 2 L at each dose, followed by colonoscopy, or a smaller volume of preparation (as little as 1 to 2 L of PEG or 300 mL of a prep containing sodium sulfate, potassium sulfate, and magnesium sulfate) taken within 4 hours of the procedure is equally effective and is better tolerated. Using a clear cap and a water jet during colonoscopy greatly improves the yield of finding stigmata of recent bleeding that may require treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r41">[41]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with massive GI bleeding presenting with hematochezia and hemodynamic instability are likely to have upper GI causes such as bleeding peptic ulcer disease, esophageal varices, or tumors. Therefore, an esophogogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) should be performed before a colonoscopy. A blood urea nitrogen (BUN) level&#x000a0;of 21 mg/dL or greater&#x000a0;is considered the best predictor of upper GI bleeding as the cause of hematochezia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r42">[42]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Small bowel capsule endoscopy and double-balloon enteroscopy are indicated for diagnosis and therapeutic intervention when bleeding is thought to arise from the small bowel.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>Radiographic Evaluation</bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>CT angiography (CTA) is the diagnostic test of choice if active bleeding is occurring at a rate of at least 0.3 mL/min or if the patient is too unstable for endoscopic intervention. CTA has a sensitivity of 85% and a specificity of 92% for identifying sites of lower GI bleeding, but it is limited to diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;If a CTA shows a bleeding site in the upper GI tract, upper endoscopy should be performed.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tagged red blood cell scintigraphy with technetium-99m sulfur colloid is an alternative means of diagnosing active bleeds, and it can detect a bleeding rate as low as 0.05 to 0.2 mL/min, making it a superior modality of investigation to diagnose GI bleeding. However, the precise localization of the bleeding site and the probable etiology are inferior to those of CTA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r44">[44]</xref> For these reasons, tagged red blood cell scintigraphy has been supplanted by CTA in most cases of massive rectal bleeding, except in patients with renal insufficiency who cannot safely receive intravenous contrast. The radionuclide scan is best used to prepare for selective angiography, colonoscopy, or small-bowel endoscopy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Contrast-enhanced CT (CECT) is the most important diagnostic tool for moderate, occult, or obscure GI bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r45">[45]</xref> CECT can readily diagnose various causes of hematochezia, including diverticulosis, malignant neoplasms of the colon, inflammatory bowel disease, and ischemic, infectious, and radiation colitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r47">[47]</xref> CECT is superior to radionuclide scans in accurately diagnosing the anatomical segment of a bleeding lesion and can provide additional information, such as staging of malignant neoplasms, the complexity of diverticular diseases, including phlegmon and abscess, and complications of IBD, including strictures, fistulization, and obstruction.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRI can be used in patients with contraindications for CECT and, rarely, if CT is inconclusive. The modalities include MR angiogram and MR enterography.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r48">[48]</xref> MRI is superior to CECT in diagnosing rectal pathologies such as bleeding polyps, solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, and rectal carcinoma.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Massive Rectal Bleeding</bold>
</p>
        <p>In massive rectal bleeding, the primary aim is to achieve hemodynamic stability by replacing blood volume and providing inotropic support as required while evaluating the etiology of the bleeding. Rectal bleeding severe enough to compromise the hemodynamic system is rare and is usually due to severe upper GI bleeding, such as from esophageal varices, peptic ulcer disease, or an aortoenteric fistula. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30795">Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding</ext-link>," for further information. Patients should receive blood transfusions to target a hemoglobin level of 7 g/dL, since endoscopic evaluation may miss smaller lesions that cause bleeding in patients with pale mucosa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r32">[32]</xref> Thresholds for packed red blood cell&#x000a0;transfusion need to be liberalized in unstable patients with active bleeding, since the initial hemoglobin level may not reflect the severity of the bleeding.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Patients taking anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs:&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The American College of Gastroenterology makes the following recommendations for patients admitted with rectal bleeding who&#x000a0;are&#x000a0;taking&#x000a0;anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Warfarin should be reversed in the presence of a life-threatening lower GI bleeding and a significantly prolonged prothrombin time by administering vitamin K, clotting factors, and fresh frozen plasma as necessary. Endoscopic intervention to achieve hemostasis can be done safely without a reversal if the international normalized ratio is 2.5 or less. Patients requiring reversal of anticoagulation should preferably receive 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC, a blood product that provides clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X), if available, as it rapidly reduces the international normalized ratio compared with fresh frozen plasma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Direct-acting oral anticoagulants, including apixaban, dabigatran, edoxaban, and rivaroxaban,&#x000a0;should be reversed&#x000a0;in the presence of life-threatening&#x000a0;lower GI bleeding that&#x000a0;fails to respond to initial resuscitation and cessation of the anticoagulant alone. If available, specific reversal agents should be used, such as idarucizumab for patients taking dabigatran.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aspirin and other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs should be discontinued after hospitalization for diverticular hemorrhage due to the risk of recurrent bleeding. However, aspirin should be continued in patients with known cardiovascular disease, since aspirin reduces the risk of further ischemic episodes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The decision to continue clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticagrelor should be addressed in an interdisciplinary setting after a significant diverticular bleeding, given the demonstrated risk of recurrent bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r49">[49]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Active bleeding identified on CTA should be treated with&#x000a0;transcatheter angiography by coiling or embolization of the involved vessel. The success rates of angiographic embolization are higher when performed within 90 minutes of a CTA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;Alternatively, colonoscopy can be attempted after a positive CTA, although blood in the lumen and an unprepared colon may obscure the bleeding site. If bleeding has stopped, a follow-up colonoscopy&#x000a0;can be performed during the same hospitalization or subsequently on an outpatient basis, depending on the patient's general condition and Oakland score.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Diverticular Bleeding</bold>
</p>
        <p>Mild&#x000a0;diverticular bleeding is usually self-limited, but severe bouts require colonoscopic, interventional radiological, or surgical interventions. The first line therapeutic modality is endoscopic&#x000a0;clipping or band ligation. In acute and severe cases with persistent bleeding that cannot be controlled with endoscopic procedures, selective transcatheter angiography is indicated. Colectomy (partial, subtotal, or total) may be necessary in refractory cases, depending on the bleeding site, the presence of diverticular disease in other segments of the colon, and the patient's comorbidities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r52">[52]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Postpolypectomy Bleeding</bold>
</p>
        <p>Postpolypectomy bleeding can occur at any time from the colonoscopy up to 14 days later. Urgent colonoscopy is usually performed in cases of severe active bleeding and may include metal clips or other modalities. Additional hemostatic modalities include cauterization with the hot tip of a polypectomy snare, a bipolar circumactive probe (BICAP), a heater probe, or noncontact argon plasma coagulation (APC). Hemostatic powder (TC-325) has also proven highly effective in this setting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;Rarely, selective angiographic embolization&#x000a0;is indicated in the setting of massive or persistent bleeding with hemodynamic instability or bleeding refractory to endoscopic treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r53">[53]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Colorectal Cancers</bold>
</p>
        <p>Management is based on the tumor's local extent, stage, and the patient's fitness for radical colorectal resection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r54">[54]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0; If operable, oncological resection should be performed, which&#x000a0;may be&#x000a0;hemicolectomy, subtotal colectomy, sigmoidectomy, anterior resection, low-anterior resection, or abdominoperineal excision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r56">[56]</xref>&#x000a0;In inoperable tumors, endoscopic interventions or hemostatic radiotherapy can be attempted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r57">[57]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radiation Colitis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Initial management should be conservative, including diet modifications, sucralfate or corticosteroid enemas, and antidiarrheal agents. The other options are formaldehyde solution, laser ablation, radiofrequency ablation, and APC.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r58">[58]</xref> In severe, refractory cases, diversion colostomy may be considered a last resort, with a reported success rate of 93%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r59">[59]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hemorrhoids</bold></p>
        <p>Management of hemorrhoids can be divided into conservative management, office-based procedures, and&#x000a0;surgical procedures.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Conservative management:</bold>
</p>
        <p>Conservative management focuses on incorporating a high-fiber diet to minimize the risk of constipation and, hence, straining while defecating. A daily intake of 25 grams of fiber for women and 38 grams for men is recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r60">[60]</xref>&#x000a0;Increasing fluid intake is also important to prevent constipation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;Stool softeners, bulk-forming laxatives, and hyperosmolar supplements, such as liquid paraffin, psyllium husk, oral milk of magnesia, and polyethylene glycol, can be used as adjuncts. Sitz baths help to decrease pain, burning, and itching following a bowel movement for active, symptomatic hemorrhoids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r62">[62]</xref>&#x000a0;Various topical local anesthetics, corticosteroids, and anti-inflammatory drugs can be used to relieve symptoms, although the efficacy of topical corticosteroids is currently unproven.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;The use of micronized purified flavonoid fraction, such as daflon, has been shown to achieve bleeding control and improve hemorrhoid-related symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r64">[64]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Office-based procedures:</bold>
</p>
        <p>Office-based procedures include rubber band ligation and sclerotherapy. These are recommended for grade II and III internal hemorrhoids with or without bleeding. The procedure involves applying a rubber band at the apex of the internal hemorrhoids with an applicator, causing the hemorrhoidal mass to necrose and slough over the next few days.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r65">[65]</xref>&#x000a0;However, 18%&#x000a0;to 32% of patients will have recurrence requiring repeated treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r66">[66]</xref>&#x000a0; Sclerotherapy involves injecting a sclerosant into the apex of the hemorrhoidal tissue, thereby inducing thrombosis and reducing hemorrhoidal bleeding and size.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r67">[67]</xref> Results from studies showed that sclerotherapy is inferior to rubber band ligation for grade III hemorrhoids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r68">[68]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Operative treatment:</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Nonexcisional procedures:</bold> Infrared coagulation, radiofrequency ablation, and laser ablation are commonly used energy ablation methods for treating internal hemorrhoids. Nonexcisional procedures work by inducing protein coagulation and local inflammation, which eventually leads to tissue necrosis and scarring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r69">[69]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r70">[70]</xref> Nonexcisional procedures are most effective for the treatment of grade II and III hemorrhoids, but nonexcisional procedures are less effective for prolapsed hemorrhoids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r71">[71]</xref> Laser hemorrhoidoplasty is commonly employed for grade II&#x000a0;to IV&#x000a0;hemorrhoids. Results from studies showed shorter operative time, 96% symptom remission, and 12% postoperative complications.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r72">[72]</xref> Doppler-guided hemorrhoidal arterial ligation has also proven effective in controlling severe and persistent hemorrhoidal bleeding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r73">[73]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Excisional procedures:</bold> Excisional procedures are reserved for patients with prolapsed, thrombosed, and strangulated hemorrhoids, and those who fail nonexcisional treatments.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r74">[74]</xref>&#x000a0;Hemorrhoidectomy can be open (Milligan-Morgan), closed (Freguson), or stapled (Longo). Closed excisional hemorrhoidectomy is the most commonly preferred operation in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r75">[75]</xref> An elliptical or a V-shaped incision is made around the hemorrhoidal tissue, which is then dissected off the underlying internal sphincter fibers in the submucosal plane to include the plexus of blood vessels. The base is then ligated with an absorbable suture, and the defect is closed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r76">[76]</xref>&#x000a0;The Milligan-Morgan technique involves a similar dissection of the hemorrhoidal tissue, but without closing the defect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r63">[63]</xref> Results from multiple studies showed that open hemorrhoidectomy is quicker, whereas closed hemorrhoidectomy results in faster wound healing, with no difference in hospital stay, postoperative pain, or complications. Traditional procedures such as anal stretch and sphincterotomy have been abandoned due to lower efficacy and increased complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r74">[74]</xref></p>
        <p>Stapled hemorrhoidectomy is another alternative procedure for treating second and third-degree hemorrhoids. This technique involves placing a purse-string suture into the submucosa above the anal transition zone. The tissue above the suture is then excised using a transplant stapler or a procedure for prolapsed hemorrhoids gun. In women, a vaginal examination should be done before firing the stapler to ensure that the posterior vaginal wall is not incorporated in the suture line.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Complications after hemorrhoidectomy:</bold>
</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;immediate complications include pain, urinary retention, hemorrhage, infection, and fecal incontinence. Topical agents such as nitroglycerin and metronidazole have been shown to decrease posthemorrhoidectomy pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r77">[77]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r78">[78]</xref>&#x000a0;Urinary retention is another common complication that can increase morbidity in patients undergoing hemorrhoidectomy. Severe hemorrhoidal disease, the number of quadrants excised, and high analgesia requirements increase the risk of these complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r79">[79]</xref> Bleeding can&#x000a0;be primary (during the&#x000a0;surgical procedure,&#x000a0;if the hemorrhoidal plexus retracts and bleeds), reactionary (immediate postoperative period due to slippage of the ligature), or secondary (due to infection, which causes erosion&#x000a0;of the blood vessels).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r80">[80]</xref>&#x000a0;Postoperative infections are very rare after a hemorrhoidectomy. However, if cellulitis or an abscess develops, antibiotics and drainage are needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;The long-term complications are anal stenosis, stricture, and recurrence. Anal stenosis can occur if excessive anoderm is removed during the procedure, and can take months to years to develop.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r82">[82]</xref>&#x000a0;Recurrence rates vary based on the grade of hemorrhoids, procedure, and other complications. Conservative management has the highest recurrence. Among the other procedures, studies have shown that rubber band ligation has the fewest complications but the highest recurrence rates. Excisional hemorrhoidectomy has the lowest recurrence rates and is currently the gold standard therapeutic procedure for bleeding hemorrhoids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r83">[83]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Anal Fissures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Conservative treatment includes stool softeners, nitroglycerin or diltiazem gel to&#x000a0;relax the anal sphincter, and warm sitz baths. Surgical management with a&#x000a0;lateral internal anal sphincterotomy may be&#x000a0;needed in chronic or refractory cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r75">[75]</xref>&#x000a0;Radiofrequency ablation, laser sphincterotomy, and botulinum injection are other available procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r84">[84]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r85">[85]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The most common differential diagnoses for rectal bleeding include anorectal causes such as hemorrhoids and anal fissures. Additional differential diagnoses include the following:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Diverticular bleeding:&#x000a0;Common in older adults and associated with chronic constipation, abdominal discomfort, and pain. CECT and colonoscopy are diagnostic.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Colorectal cancers: Present with hematochezia, altered bowel habits, passage of mucus, spurious diarrhea, and constitutional symptoms such as weight loss and cachexia. CT, MRI, and colonoscopy are useful for diagnosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammatory bowel diseases:&#x000a0;Ulcerative colitis, in particular, can present with rectal bleeding associated with abdominal pain and altered bowel habits.&#x000a0;Colonoscopy and biopsy can aid in diagnosing and&#x000a0;monitoring the condition.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ischemic colitis:&#x000a0;Presents with an acute onset of abdominal pain and rectal bleeding. Patients are usually&#x000a0;older adults with cardiovascular diseases. The diagnosis can be confirmed with a CECT and colonoscopy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Angiodysplasias</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adenomas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infectious colitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Radiation colitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Drug-induced bleeding&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis depends on the cause of rectal bleeding, the severity, and the patient's underlying health. Approximately 95% of rectal bleeding cases will regress spontaneously, but severe or protracted bleeding may cause ischemic compromise of various organs. The in-hospital mortality ranges from 1% to 25%. The independent predictors of mortality include older age, ischemic colitis, 2 or more comorbid illnesses, coagulopathy, hypovolemia, need for packed red blood cell&#x000a0;transfusion, and male sex. Hemorrhoidal disease and colorectal polyps carry a low risk of mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-81086.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of rectal bleeding can be due to the severity of bleeding and the underlying disease process. Massive ongoing bleeding can result in circulatory collapse and features of compromised end-organ perfusion such as acute renal failure, altered sensorium, transaminitis, and hypovolemic shock. Recurrent mild to moderate rectal bleeding leads to chronic anemia, often presenting as iron deficiency anemia.&#x000a0;Complications of the underlying disease processes are specific to the primary etiology:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Diverticulosis:</bold> Diverticulitis, perforation, peritonitis, and intra-abdominal abscess</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hemorrhoids:</bold> Irreducibility, thrombosis, and strangulation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Colorectal polyps:</bold> Malignant transformation and intestinal obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Colorectal cancers:</bold> Large bowel obstruction, perforation, infiltration of adjacent organs, and distant metastasis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ischemic colitis:</bold> Colonic perforation and peritonitis, gram-negative septicemia, and shock</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Radiation colitis:</bold> Recurrent abdominal pain and intestinal obstruction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Inflammatory bowel disease:</bold> Frequent relapses, fulminant colitis, toxic megacolon, perforation, stricture, and malignant transformation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Rectal bleeding is a common clinical presentation, yet it is often overlooked or attributed to benign pathology. Although most causes of rectal bleeding are benign and self-limiting, patient awareness regarding more complex and serious etiologies is crucial. High-risk populations such as older adults, patients with a family history of colorectal cancer, patients with previously diagnosed colorectal polyps, patients with inflammatory bowel disease, bleeding disorders, and anticoagulant use should be thoroughly educated. Appropriate referral pathways should be initiated, and patients must be directed accordingly to ensure a complete evaluation. Clinicians should also emphasize the role of colonoscopy as a screening tool during regular consultations.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Appropriate management and patient-centered care for rectal bleeding require collaborative efforts among health care professionals. Patients with rectal bleeding may exhibit nonspecific signs and symptoms, and therefore, practicing clinicians must equip themselves with adequate knowledge regarding the various etiologies, symptomatology, and complications of rectal bleeding. Interdisciplinary team communication and patient care coordination between primary care clinicians, gastroenterologists, general and colorectal surgeons, radiologists, nursing professionals, and endoscopic technicians are critical in optimizing patient outcomes. Effective interdisciplinary communication creates a constructive environment where information is shared, questions are encouraged, and concerns are addressed promptly. A strategic approach involving evidence-based guidelines and individualized care plans tailored to each patient's unique circumstances is vital. Care coordination supports optimal patient outcomes, making the patient's journey seamless and patient-centered. Through improved interdisciplinary communication and coordinated management plans, clinicians will enhance their competency in accurately diagnosing and effectively treating patients with rectal bleeding.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-81086.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
